# Agile Project Management Tool

This project is a simple full-stack application built to manage work for a small team using an agile-style workflow.

The main goal was not to build something complex, but to design a system that:
- clearly structures work
- tracks progress across levels
- enforces role-based control

Work is organized in three levels:

Project → Story → Task

Each level represents a different level of abstraction, from overall goals down to individual tasks.
---

## Why this was built this way

Instead of focusing on using complex frameworks, the focus was on:

* clear data relationships
* clean backend logic
* complete functionality

The idea was to build something that works end-to-end and is easy to understand and extend.

---

## Tech Stack

* Backend: Flask
* Database: SQLite
* ORM: SQLAlchemy
* Authentication: Flask-Login
* Frontend: HTML + Bootstrap

---

## Features

### User roles

There are three types of users:

* Admin
* Project Manager
* Viewer

Permissions are controlled based on role:

* Admin manages roles
* Manager creates and updates projects, stories, and tasks
* Viewer can only view data

---

### Work hierarchy

Work is structured as:

* A project contains multiple stories
* A story contains multiple tasks

This keeps everything organized and easy to navigate.

---

### Task management

* Tasks can be created under stories
* Tasks can be assigned to users
* Each task has a status:

  * To Do
  * In Progress
  * Done

---

### Automatic progress tracking

The system updates progress automatically:

* If all tasks in a story are done → story is marked as Done
* If all stories in a project are done → project is marked as Done

---

### Due dates and overdue handling

* Projects have due dates
* If a project is not completed before its due date → it is marked as Overdue

---

### Background processing (Async)

A background thread runs periodically and checks:

* project deadlines
* updates project status if needed

This is a simple implementation of async behavior.

---

## Project structure

```
agile-project/
│
├── app.py          # App setup and background thread
├── models.py       # Database models
├── routes.py       # Application logic and routes
├── templates/      # HTML pages
├── static/         # Styling files
├── seed.py         # Reset and seed database
└── requirements.txt
```

---

## Database design

### User

* id
* username
* password
* role

### Project

* id
* name
* description
* due_date
* status

### Story

* id
* title
* status
* project_id

### Task

* id
* title
* status
* story_id
* assigned_to

---

## Key routes

| Method   | Endpoint                        | Purpose              |
| -------- | ------------------------------- | -------------------- |
| GET      | /login                          | Login page           |
| POST     | /login                          | Login user           |
| GET      | /dashboard                      | Main dashboard       |
| GET/POST | /create_project                 | Create project       |
| GET      | /view_projects                  | View all projects    |
| GET      | /project/<id>                   | View project details |
| GET/POST | /create_story/<project_id>      | Create story         |
| GET      | /story/<id>                     | View story           |
| GET/POST | /create_task/<story_id>         | Create task          |
| GET      | /update_task/<task_id>/<status> | Update task status   |

---

## How to run the project

1. Clone the repository

```
git clone <repo-link>
cd agile-project
```

2. Create virtual environment

```
python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate
```

3. Install dependencies

```
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

4. Initialize database

```
python seed.py
```

5. Run the app

```
python app.py
```

6. Open in browser

```
http://127.0.0.1:5000
```

---

## Design decisions

* Flask was used to keep the backend simple and understandable
* SQLite was chosen for quick setup and no external dependency
* A background thread was used instead of Celery to keep the system lightweight
* UI was kept minimal using Bootstrap to focus on functionality

---

## Security notes

* Role-based access control is implemented
* Routes are protected using login sessions

Limitations:

* Passwords are stored in plain text (can be improved using hashing)
* No advanced authentication mechanisms

---

## What can be improved

* Add password hashing
* Add real-time updates
* Add notifications or reminders
* Improve UI further
* Replace background thread with a proper task queue

---

## AI usage

AI was used during development for:

* debugging
* structuring the project
* improving implementation speed

All logic and concepts were understood and verified before implementation.

---

## Final note

The focus of this project was not to build something complex, but to build something complete, structured, and easy to understand.
